<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Xingyang</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Xingyang is a county-level city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a> province, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhengzhou">Zhengzhou</a> near the southern bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a>. Its position at the eastern end of the hill country guarding the approaches to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> made it strategically important in early Chinese history, most famously during the Chu–Han contention, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bang">Liu Bang</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiang-yu">Xiang Yu</a> fought long campaigns around Xingyang and the nearby Ao granary.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/xingyang.jpg" alt="Xingyang" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Windmemories · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Crossing_of_Guangwu_Rd._and_Suohe_Rd._in_Xingyang_20200520.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
